# Patent Claim

**Claim 1.** A hardware acceleration system for network stream processing, comprising:

a network interface configured to receive and transmit data packets from a communication network operating according to at least one network protocol;

an accelerator module operatively coupled between the network interface and a host system, the accelerator module comprising:

  (a) a packet classification engine configured to parse incoming packet headers and classify each packet according to protocol type, flow identifier, and quality-of-service designation;

  (b) a hardware-implemented protocol offload circuit configured to perform, independent of host system processor intervention, at least one of checksum computation and validation, packet segmentation and reassembly, and encryption or decryption operations upon classified packet data;

  (c) a buffer memory configured to temporarily store packet data during processing by the protocol offload circuit; and

  (d) a direct memory access (DMA) engine configured to transfer processed packet data between the buffer memory and a host memory associated with the host system without requiring intermediate copying through the host system processor;

a controller unit communicatively coupled to the accelerator module, the controller unit configured to:

  (i) receive configuration parameters from the host system defining processing rules applicable to the packet classification engine and the protocol offload circuit;

  (ii) monitor operational status of the accelerator module, including buffer occupancy levels and processing throughput metrics;

  (iii) generate and issue interrupt signals to the host system upon occurrence of predetermined events, the predetermined events including completion of a packet processing operation and detection of an error condition; and

  (iv) dynamically adjust processing parameters of the accelerator module based on the monitored operational status; and

a data bus interface disposed between the accelerator module and the host system, the data bus interface configured to:

  (I) transmit the processed packet data from the DMA engine to the host memory via a host system bus protocol;

  (II) transmit control and configuration signals between the controller unit and the host system; and

  (III) support burst-mode data transfer between the accelerator module and the host memory to reduce transfer latency;

wherein the accelerator module, the controller unit, and the data bus interface cooperate to offload network stream processing operations from a host processor of the host system, thereby reducing host processor utilization during network data transfer.

---

**Claim 2.** The system of claim 1, wherein the protocol offload circuit further comprises a hardware-implemented deep packet inspection engine configured to analyze payload content of the classified packets according to a set of pattern-matching rules stored in the buffer memory.

**Claim 3.** The system of claim 1, wherein the controller unit further comprises a scheduling circuit configured to prioritize processing of packets classified as belonging to a higher-priority flow over packets classified as belonging to a lower-priority flow.

**Claim 4.** The system of claim 1, wherein the data bus interface comprises a Peripheral Component Interconnect Express (PCIe) interface, and wherein the burst-mode data transfer is performed using PCIe transaction layer packets.

**Claim 5.** The system of claim 1, wherein the DMA engine is further configured to support scatter-gather operations enabling the transfer of processed packet data to non-contiguous regions of the host memory in a single DMA transaction.

**Claim 6.** The system of claim 1, wherein the accelerator module further comprises a hardware timestamp circuit configured to associate a timestamp value with each received data packet upon arrival at the network interface.

**Claim 7.** The system of claim 1, wherein the controller unit is further configured to dynamically reallocate buffer memory capacity among a plurality of concurrently processed network flows based on the monitored buffer occupancy levels.
